Here's my analysis of `InputValidatorTests.cs`:

---

## Test Maintainability Report

### Summary

**1 major finding:** Your 9 test methods are classic copy-paste tests — all share the same structure but differ only in input values and expected outcomes. This is the exact pain you described: each new case requires a new method.

---

### Finding: Copy-Paste Tests → Parameterized Tests

**Category:** Copy-paste test methods (all 9 methods)

Every method follows the identical pattern:
```csharp
var validator = new InputValidator();
var result = validator.Validate(INPUT);
Assert.IsX(result.IsValid);
Assert.AreEqual(EXPECTED_ERROR, result.ErrorMessage);
```

**Suggested refactoring — convert to `[DataTestMethod]` with `[DataRow]`:**

```csharp
[TestClass]
public sealed class InputValidatorTests
{
    [DataTestMethod]
    [DataRow("",        false, "Input cannot be empty",               DisplayName = "Empty string")]
    [DataRow("ab",      false, "Input must be at least 3 characters", DisplayName = "Too short")]
    [DataRow("hello<script>", false, "Input contains invalid characters", DisplayName = "Special chars")]
    [DataRow(null,      false, "Input cannot be empty",               DisplayName = "Null")]
    [DataRow("   ",    false, "Input cannot be empty",               DisplayName = "Whitespace only")]
    [DataRow("hello-world", true, null,                              DisplayName = "Valid input")]
    [DataRow("abc",    true, null,                                   DisplayName = "Min boundary")]
    public void Validate_ReturnsExpectedResult(string? input, bool expectedValid, string? expectedError)
    {
        var validator = new InputValidator();
        var result = validator.Validate(input);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedValid, result.IsValid);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedError, result.ErrorMessage);
    }

    [DataTestMethod]
    [DataRow(256, false, "Input must not exceed 255 characters", DisplayName = "Too long")]
    [DataRow(255, true,  null,                                   DisplayName = "Max boundary")]
    public void Validate_LengthBoundary_ReturnsExpectedResult(int length, bool expectedValid, string? expectedError)
    {
        var validator = new InputValidator();
        var result = validator.Validate(new string('a', length));
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedValid, result.IsValid);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedError, result.ErrorMessage);
    }
}
```

---

### Impact

| Metric | Before | After |
|--------|--------|-------|
| Methods | 9 | 2 |
| Lines | ~80 | ~30 |
| Adding a new case | New 7-line method | One `[DataRow]` line |

### Trade-offs

- **Gained:** Adding a test case is now a single `[DataRow]` attribute — no new method needed.
- **Lost:** Individual test bodies are no longer visible at a glance per case (mitigated by `DisplayName`).
- **Note:** The two separate methods keep string-literal inputs apart from generated-string inputs, since `[DataRow]` requires compile-time constants.

Would you like me to apply this refactoring to the file?
